<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Troupes de la Marine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Troupes de la Marine were independent companies of infantry under the French Ministry of the Marine, raised to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s colonies and naval ports. From 1683 they formed the regular garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, and many of their officers were Canadian-born men skilled in frontier warfare alongside Native allies. They were reorganised in 1761 after the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troupes-de-la-marine.jpg" alt="Troupes de la Marine" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: French School · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Disembarkation_of_50_soldiers_from_the_Karrer_Swiss_regiment_at_Louisbourg_on_the_Island_of_Cap-Breton_between_1722-33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the 17th century soldiers serving at sea were seconded from the French Army. In 1622 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> created the first French troops for maritime service, the Compagnies Ordinaires de la Mer, intended as marines on the ships of the French Navy, and soon merged them into the Régiment de La Marine, which fought at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>. Further naval regiments followed during the Franco-Spanish War, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> raised two more in 1669, but all were eventually transferred to the Army between 1640 and 1673.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> an ordinance of 16 December 1690 created 80 infantry companies under the name Compagnies Franches de la Marine, each commanded by a lieutenant de vaisseau. Their strength rose and fell with war and the budget: 100 companies of 100 men in theory from 1691, 50 men after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Treaty of Ryswick</a> in 1697, 100 again in 1705, and repeated cuts after the Treaty of Utrecht.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The companies guarded arsenals and naval ports, served aboard warships and garrisoned the colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the Antilles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Some charged at the Battle of Camaret in 1694; at the Battle of Málaga in 1704 they lost 150 officers and 1,500 soldiers; and 2,500 of their men fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> under René Duguay-Trouin in 1711.</p><p>Between 1683 and 1688 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> sent thirty-five companies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and they remained the only regular troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> until 1755. In 1688 Canada held 1,418 soldiers for a population of almost 10,300. From 1685 Canadian gentlemen obtained commissions, and by 1722 more than half the officers had been born in Canada; a commission was a source of income and prestige, with a waiting list. With the Canadian militias and allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> units the troops bore the defence of New France, and at the end of the French regime 2,600 of them were in Canada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the conquest of 1760 many veterans settled in the new territory, while others were reluctantly repatriated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1762 the duties of the companies passed to 23 line regiments and three artillery <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>. In 1769 César Gabriel de Choiseul founded the Corps royal d'artillerie et d'infanterie de la marine, and further reorganisations followed under the monarchy, the Republic and the Empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Troupes de la Marine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New France was almost always outnumbered by its English rivals and depended on Native alliances, a militia that included all able-bodied men, and from the 1680s the <b>Troupes de la Marine</b>, regular soldiers under the navy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
